    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Is hip-hop music exclusively for black people? Invented by blacks and latinos in the South Bronx in the early 1970's, hip-hop and rap have grown and expanded all over the world. Should we, then, consider hip hop to be something that blacks \"own\", or should this consideration be tossed. In this podcast, I highlight this dilemma in a number of ways. I begin by documenting a recent event in hip-hop culture in which one of the genre's early artists label white rappers as \"guests in the house of hip-hop\". Next, I discuss the history and development of hip-hop, and conclude with my personal experience, as a white person, with hip-hop.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
    ],
